{"reference":"Hebrews 12","book":"Hebrews","chapter":12,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter opens by linking a “cloud of witnesses,” laying aside what encumbers, running with endurance, and fixing attention on Jesus. The imagery presents endurance as a sustained course rather than a momentary effort.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 12:1-3"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_012_3dbc957aeb248f654d201308"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading Difficult Passages","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verses 4–13 use parental discipline to interpret hardship and then call the weakened to be strengthened. This parental metaphor should not be used to justify abuse, corporal violence, blaming victims, ableism, medical neglect, or demands that someone remain in danger.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 12:14-17","Hebrews 12:18-24","Hebrews 12:25-29","Hebrews 12:4-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_012_cb0ffa1679f0324aba605fcb"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"Esau serves here as a warning figure drawn through the reception of Genesis; the passage does not offer a complete doctrine of repentance or authorize contempt for Esau’s descendants. Likewise, the contrast between the fearful mountain scene and Zion, the heavenly Jerusalem, is a rhetorical contrast between scriptural scenes, not a contrast between evil Judaism and good Christianity.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 12:14-17","Hebrews 12:18-24","Hebrews 12:25-29","Hebrews 12:4-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_012_cb0ffa1679f0324aba605fcb"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The closing verses explicitly quote and interpret Haggai’s oracle about shaking. Hebrews connects that shaking with the removal of what can be shaken, the permanence of what cannot, acceptable worship, divine fire, and the reception of an unshakable kingdom. Its kingdom language is framed by gratitude, reverence, and awe, not as support for nationalism, theocracy, imperial domination, or religious violence.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 12:26-29","Hebrews 12:25-29","Hebrews 12:14-17","Hebrews 12:18-24","Hebrews 12:4-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_012_afbd4f7a176abaf536a9a26e","reader_path_hebrews_012_9753235dd2ec29d922df9b20","reader_path_hebrews_012_cb0ffa1679f0324aba605fcb"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Textual Transmission","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Papyrus 46 is an early substantial witness to Hebrews, although it contains gaps and its date can only be estimated paleographically. Codex Sinaiticus also preserves Hebrews in its New Testament sequence after 2 Thessalonians.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_012_f7341f50ff8acfcd9a24e43a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}